<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · city</p><h1 class="enc-title">Eisleben</h1><p class="enc-meta">Also known as Lutherstadt Eisleben</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Eisleben, officially Lutherstadt Eisleben, is a town in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/germany">Germany</a>, east of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/harz">Harz</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> was born there in 1483 and died there in 1546, and the houses of his birth and death are <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Sites</a>. The town belonged to the counts of Mansfeld and was for centuries a centre of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/copper">copper</a> mining in the Mansfeld region.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/eisleben.jpg" alt="Eisleben" width="320" height="180" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Frans Bosch · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Lutherstadt_Eisleben_Markt.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Eisleben was first mentioned in 997 as a market called Islebia, and in 1180 as a town. It is divided into the old town, the Altstadt, and the Neustadt, created in the fourteenth century for the town's miners. The counts of Mansfeld governed the area until the eighteenth century. In the Reformation Count Hoyer VI of Mansfeld-Vorderort stayed loyal to the Catholic faith, while the Mittelort and Hinterort branches of the family sided with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a>. Count Albert VII of Mansfeld-Hinterort signed the Protestant Augsburg Confession in 1530 and joined the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/schmalkaldic-league">Schmalkaldic League</a>. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/german-peasants-war">German Peasants' War</a> devastated the area.</p><p>In 1574 the surviving Mansfeld counts agreed with the Elector of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony">Saxony</a> to repay the family's large debts, though some properties were forfeited by 1579. The Hinterort branches died out in 1666 and the Mansfeld-Vorderort line in 1780, when Eisleben came directly under the Electorate of Saxony. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/napoleonic-wars">Napoleonic Wars</a> the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/congress-of-vienna">Congress of Vienna</a> assigned it to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-prussia">Kingdom of Prussia</a>. In 1947 it became part of the new state of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saxony-anhalt">Saxony-Anhalt</a>, and from 1952 it belonged to the Bezirk Halle in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/east-germany">German Democratic Republic</a>. It rejoined the re-created Saxony-Anhalt after reunification in 1990. On 6 June 1927 the American aviator Clarence D. Chamberlin landed in a wheat field outside the town, completing the first transatlantic passenger flight.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/martin-luther">Martin Luther</a> was born in Eisleben on 10 November 1483. His father, Hans Luther, was a miner, like many in the town, and the family moved to Mansfeld when Luther was a year old. He lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wittenberg">Wittenberg</a> for most of his life, but he preached his last sermon in Eisleben and died there in 1546. The town took steps to preserve its Luther memorials as early as 1689 and pioneered heritage tourism. In 1997 Luther's birth house and death house were designated a <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/unesco-world-heritage-site">UNESCO World Heritage Site</a>, together with the Luther sites in Wittenberg. Luther was baptised in the town's church of Saint Peter and Paul, whose original font survives, and he preached his last sermons at Saint Andreas Church, both still in use.</p><p>At Helfta, now a district of the town, the counts of Mansfeld founded a monastery in 1258. It became known for powerful and mystical abbesses, among them Gertrude of Hackeborn, Gertrude the Great and Mechtild of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/magdeburg">Magdeburg</a>, and was sometimes called the crown of German convents. It was destroyed in 1342, rebuilt the next year and secularised after 1542. Cistercian nuns have lived in the restored cloister since about 1999. The town had 22,668 inhabitants in 2020.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Eisleben” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Martin Luther (1483–1546), a mining entrepreneur’s son from <b>Eisleben</b>, had abandoned the study of law in 1505 for the Augustinian friary at Erfurt.</blockquote><a href="/movement/protestant-reformation">Protestant Reformation · People</a></li><li><blockquote>A line of its last stanza is inscribed on a memorial at Osterhausen near <b>Eisleben</b> in Saxony-Anhalt that commemorates both the Peasants' War of 1525 and the land reform of 1945, which the memorial's designers saw as fulfilling the peasants' cause generations later.</blockquote><a href="/songs/wir-sind-des-geyers-schwarzer-haufen">Wir sind des Geyers schwarzer Haufen</a></li><li><blockquote>Martin Luther was born in nearby <b>Eisleben</b> in 1483 and grew up in Mansfeld, where his father ran smelters.</blockquote><a href="/state/county-of-mansfeld">County of Mansfeld</a></li></ul></section>
